B	MS-7E06	7E06vAF
K	0	Hyper-Threading	Lets each P-core run two threads at once. Adds throughput in heavily multi-threaded work (rendering, compiling, encoding); how much depends on the program, so time a run of your own workload with it on and off. It does not make a single thread faster. Turning it off can lower temperatures a little and sometimes lets a P-core clock higher.	Enabled	Enabled	~Either (off can run cooler/clock higher)	Enabled	~Test both. Off is often equal or better in high-fps shooters		Often neutral, sometimes slightly better 1% lows with HT off on 12th-14th gen. Fewer threads then share each P-core's cache and execution units. It varies by title, so test it.	Heavily threaded games (big open worlds, city builders, shader compilation) can lose a few percent or stutter more with HT off, especially if E-cores are also off.	Rendering, compiling and encoding run noticeably slower with HT off. Time your own job both ways to see by how much.	The vendor line "HT is free extra performance" holds for throughput work. On CPUs with many cores it is often a wash in games; test it. The tweak-guide line "HT off always helps games" is just as overstated.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
K	1	Intel Turbo Boost	Allows the CPU to run above its base clock when there is power and thermal headroom. Ignored when you set a fixed all-core ratio (CPU Ratio Mode = Fixed).	~Disabled caps the CPU at base clock (biggest power saving)	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	~Either. No effect with a fixed ratio (recommended for this goal)	With a fixed P-Core Ratio this switch has no effect.	Large effect at stock ratios (an i7-14700K, for example, is rated up to 5.6 GHz turbo against a 3.4 GHz P-core base). None when a fixed all-core ratio is set.	Same as FPS titles.	Same as games.	Accurate.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	up to 5.6 GHzpublishedhttps://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/236783/x/specifications.html3.4 GHzpublishedhttps://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/236783/x/specifications.html
K	2	Turbo Boost Max 3.0	Identifies the best P-cores and runs single-threaded work on them at a higher clock.	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	~Either. No effect with a fixed ratio (recommended for this goal)	No effect under a fixed all-core ratio.	Small. Games use more than the two favoured cores. None under a fixed ratio.	Small.	Lightly threaded apps gain one speed bin on the favoured cores (Intel lists 5.6 GHz with Turbo Boost Max 3.0 vs 5.5 GHz P-core max turbo for an i7-14700K).	Marketing makes it sound bigger than it is. On an i7-14700K the favoured cores are about 100 MHz faster (Intel's spec page lists both clocks).	published	https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/236783/x/specifications.html	5.6 GHzpublishedhttps://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/236783/x/specifications.html5.5 GHzpublishedhttps://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/236783/x/specifications.htmlabout 100 MHzpublishedhttps://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/236783/x/specifications.html
K	3	EIST (SpeedStep)	Lets the CPU drop its clock and voltage when lightly loaded. Saves a lot of idle power. Turning it off keeps clocks high at all times.	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	~Either. No effect with a fixed ratio		Under game load the busy cores already sit at max clock, so average fps barely changes. Turning it off can remove brief clock dips on lightly loaded threads, which is a small effect on lows at best.	Negligible.	Negligible under load. Off raises idle power and heat noticeably.	"Disable EIST for fps" is mostly folklore since Speed Shift arrived. With a fixed ratio it does nothing either way.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
K	4	Intel Speed Shift (HWP)	Hardware-controlled clock changes. The CPU changes clock much faster than with Windows-controlled P-states, so it is both more efficient and more responsive.	Enabled	Auto	Enabled	Enabled	~Enabled (no effect with a fixed ratio and EIST off)	Disabling it falls back to slower OS-controlled clock changes.	Little effect under sustained game load. Helps bursty threads clock up fast when clocks are dynamic.	Little effect.	Snappier desktop and bursty tasks when clocks are dynamic.	Accurate for dynamic clocks. Irrelevant with a fixed ratio and EIST off.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
K	5	CPU C-States	Allows idle cores to power down. Saves idle power and heat, and frees power/thermal headroom for busy cores. Waking from a deep C-state takes tens of microseconds.	Enabled	Auto	Enabled	Auto	~Disabled lowers wake-up latency but raises idle power/heat	With C-states off, idle cores stay at load voltage and run warmer. The 13th/14th-gen damage came mainly from very high voltage requests during boost (Intel's 0x129 microcode limits requests above 1.55 V). A moderate fixed voltage at idle is a smaller risk, but not zero.	Off can shave microseconds of wake-up time from threads that briefly sleep. Some players measure slightly better 1% lows or input latency, but it is often within run-to-run noise. Busy game threads rarely reach deep C-states.	Usually no measurable change.	No throughput change. Idle power and temperatures rise.	Intel's "no performance cost" is true for averages and slightly overstated for latency. Tweak-guide claims of big fps gains are overstated.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	above 1.55 Vpublishedhttps://www.tomshardware.com/pc-components/cpus/intel-details-microcode-update-patch-has-negligible-performance-impacts-requires-bios-update
K	6	C1E	Lowers core voltage in the shallowest idle state.	Enabled	Auto	Auto	Auto	Disabled		Tiny wake-up gain at best.	Negligible.	None under load. Idle power goes up.	Low impact either way.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
