K	11	Race To Halt	Finishes work at high clock then sleeps; usually more efficient overall.	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled	Enabled		Minimal.	Minimal.	Slight efficiency gain.	Small either way.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
K	12	Hardware Prefetcher	Predicts which memory the CPU will need next and loads it into cache early. The effect depends on the workload. It helps code that reads memory in order: loading, decompression, encoding, most apps. It can hurt code with random access patterns, such as many game engines and databases, and all-core work that already saturates memory bandwidth. In those cases wrong guesses evict useful data and waste bandwidth. One measurement: Hadron's author got about +20% better 1% lows in Overwatch with it off on his earlier i9-9900K. That is a different CPU from his current i7-14700KF (and probably from yours), and one game. Results differ by CPU and game, so test it on yours rather than assume.	Enabled	Enabled	~Test both. Usually Enabled helps single-thread apps; some games run better Disabled	~Test both. Disabled can help bandwidth-bound all-core loads; Enabled helps streaming work (encoding, compression)	~Test both in your own games and compare 1% lows over several identical runs	How to test: same scene or replay, uncapped fps if possible, compare 1% and 0.1% lows across 3+ runs each way. AIDA64 latency alone does not tell you which is faster in games. 12th-14th gen have much larger L2 caches per core than older Intel CPUs, so a result from a 9900K may not carry over.	Can help lows a lot in some shooters and hurt in others. Hadron's author measured about +20% better 1% lows in Overwatch with it off, on an i9-9900K, a different CPU from his current i7-14700KF. Test it per game: same scene, compare 1% lows over several runs each way.	Mixed. Streaming open-world games lean towards Enabled.	Enabled usually wins for encoding, compression, compiling and loading. Off can help some bandwidth-bound all-core work.	Intel ships it on because it wins on average across benchmarks. "Always better" is overstated for random-access game engines.	owner-measured	owner's i9-9900K, Overwatch, 1% lows, about +20% with the Hardware Prefetcher disabled (a different CPU from the owner's current i7-14700KF). The rest of the entry is reasoning from how the hardware works.	about +20%owner-measuredowner's i9-9900K, Overwatch, 1% lows, about +20% with the Hardware Prefetcher disabledabout +20%owner-measuredowner's i9-9900K, Overwatch, 1% lows, about +20% with the Hardware Prefetcher disabled
K	13	Adjacent Cache Line Prefetch	Always fetches the paired 64-byte neighbour of every line the CPU requests. It helps sequential access, but on random access it doubles memory traffic for nothing. It is the prefetcher most often disabled for bandwidth-heavy servers and HPC.	Enabled	Enabled	~Test both. Usually Enabled	~Test both. Disabled often helps bandwidth-bound all-core loads	~Test both in your games. Disabled can improve 1% lows in random-access-heavy titles	Test it separately from the Hardware Prefetcher so you know which one made the difference.	Off can improve lows in random-access-heavy games, and it is game-dependent. Test it separately from the Hardware Prefetcher.	Mixed.	Enabled for most apps. Off is a common win for bandwidth-bound servers and HPC.	Same as the Hardware Prefetcher: tuned for the average benchmark, not for every game.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
