K	19	CPU Ratio Mode	Dynamic lets clocks drop when idle; Fixed holds the set ratio all the time (no ramp-up delay, but more idle heat and power).	Dynamic Mode	Dynamic Mode	Dynamic Mode	~Either	Fixed Mode	Fixed removes clock transitions, so EIST, Speed Shift, Turbo, Turbo Boost Max 3.0 and TVB stop mattering. Trade-offs: the fixed all-core ratio is usually below the CPU's single-core boost clock, so lightly threaded loads can be slower. With Override voltage, idle cores sit at load voltage, which adds heat and some wear. Thermal throttling and C-states still apply. Stability comes from enough voltage for the clock, not from fixed vs dynamic.	Fixed removes clock transitions entirely, which some players see as steadier lows. With Speed Shift on, busy cores already sit near max, so the gap is often small. Compare your fixed all-core ratio with your CPU's rated single-core boost: if it is lower, lightly threaded work runs slower. Test it: compare 1% lows with a frame-time logger over a few identical runs in each mode.	Small.	Same throughput at the same clock. Idle power is higher with Fixed.	Small effect either way.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
