K	55	PCIe LTR / PTM (per root port)	Latency Tolerance Reporting (LTR) lets each PCIe device tell the CPU's power controller how long it can wait for a memory access. The power controller uses that to decide how deep an idle state the package may enter. Snoop/Non-Snoop Latency Override makes the root port report your own fixed tolerance instead of the device's: the Value multiplied by the time unit the Multiplier selects. Force LTR Override makes your value win even when the device reports its own, and LTR Lock stops the OS changing it. PTM (Precision Time Measurement) keeps clocks in sync between the CPU and the device. These are real, changeable settings: most appear under Chipset > PCH-IO / System Agent > PCI Express Configuration > Root Port N when that port is enabled. The group repeats for every root port.	~Auto, or raise the values to allow deeper idle	~Auto	~Auto	~Auto	~Auto (no effect with C-states off)	What changing them does: these values are a tolerance report, not a speed setting. They do not make the PCIe link or the device respond faster. Lowering them stops the CPU package entering deep idle states (the same effect as limiting package C-states, but per device). Raising them allows deeper idle and saves power. With C-states disabled, the CPU never enters those states, so the values change nothing.	None measurable. Low override values only block deep package idle, which matters at idle, not under game load. With C-states off there is no effect at all.	No measurable effect.	None on performance. Raising the values lets the CPU idle deeper and save power; lowering them raises idle power.	Some tweak lists say to set Snoop/Non-Snoop latency to the minimum for lower input latency. The values are a power-management tolerance report: they cannot make a device or PCIe link respond faster. At most they keep the CPU out of deep idle, which disabling C-states already does.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
