K	99	tRC (row cycle)	Minimum time between opening two rows in the same bank: tRAS + tRP.	~Auto	~Auto (XMP value)	~Lower after XMP is stable, then test	~Lower after XMP is stable, then test	~Lower after XMP is stable, then test	Change one timing at a time and test with a memory stress test (TestMem5 with an extreme/absolut config, Karhu, or y-cruncher) for at least an hour before trusting it. Memory errors corrupt files silently.	Tiny. Rarely worth tuning by hand.	Tiny.	Tiny.		reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
K	100	tRFC (refresh cycle time)	How long a refresh blocks the RAM. DRAM must be refreshed constantly; during tRFC that memory cannot be used. Lower tRFC means shorter stalls. tRFC2/tRFCsb/tRFCpb are the same idea for finer-grained refresh modes.	~Auto	~Auto (XMP value)	~Lower after XMP is stable, then test	~Lower after XMP is stable, then test	~Lower after XMP is stable, then test	tRFC depends on the memory chips (Hynix A/M-die, Samsung, Micron) and temperature. Change one timing at a time and test with a memory stress test (TestMem5 with an extreme/absolut config, Karhu, or y-cruncher) for at least an hour before trusting it. Memory errors corrupt files silently.	One of the best subtimings for 1% lows: refresh stalls are exactly the kind of hiccup that hurts frame times. XMP values are usually very loose.	Smaller effect unless CPU-bound.	Small to moderate for memory-heavy work.	Rarely mentioned by vendors, but one of the most effective manual tweaks.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
K	101	tREFI (refresh interval)	Time between refreshes. Higher tREFI means fewer refreshes, so the RAM is blocked less often. The risk is data loss if cells leak charge before the next refresh, and that gets worse as the RAM heats up.	~Auto	~Auto	~Raise after XMP is stable, then test	~Raise after XMP is stable, then test	~Raise after XMP is stable, then test	Keep the RAM cool (a fan over the DIMMs helps). Errors from a too-high tREFI may only appear when the sticks get warm. Change one timing at a time and test with a memory stress test (TestMem5 with an extreme/absolut config, Karhu, or y-cruncher) for at least an hour before trusting it. Memory errors corrupt files silently.	Big effect on memory throughput and lows when raised from the conservative default, together with tRFC.	Moderate when CPU-bound.	Moderate for memory-heavy work.	Standard spec values are set for worst-case temperature. Most desktops run far cooler, which is why there is headroom.	reasoning	From how the hardware works; not measured. Workload-dependent effects: test them.	
